WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.000
When you think "cringe," "The Office" or "Curb&nbsp;
Your Enthusiasm" likely spring to mind, not "Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.600
Wars." But a moment in "Andor 'has viewers'&nbsp;
secondhand embarrassment alarms going wild.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.640
Spoilers for the first three&nbsp;
episodes of "Andor" ahead.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.720
"Andor" is a completely different beast from&nbsp;
the other "Star Wars" shows to come to Disney+,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.760
Unlike the other series thus far, "Andor"&nbsp;
aims for a grittier, more street-level, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.320
more solemn look at the "Star Wars" universe,&nbsp;
much in the same way as the movie "Rogue One."

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.560
This isn't exactly surprising, given that&nbsp;
"Andor" serves as a prequel to that film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.960
focusing on the title character Cassian&nbsp;
Andor. As any true "Star Wars" fan knows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.880
Cassian ends up dying at the end of&nbsp;
"Rogue One," along with all the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:44.640
main characters after they've successfully&nbsp;
stolen the plans for the Death Star.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.360
Given the darker tone of the film that inspired&nbsp;
it, and the return of "Rogue One" co-writer Tony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.800
Gilroy as the developer of "Andor," it's really no&nbsp;
wonder that the new series leans into a narrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.120
heaviness that hasn't been quite as&nbsp;
prevalent in previous Disney+ series.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.880
However, despite that seriousness,&nbsp;
the show also might feature one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.080
the most cringe-inducing scenes in the entire&nbsp;
"Star Wars" universe in its second episode.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.680
Of course, it's not like "cringe" is new to&nbsp;
"Star Wars" – but this one is on purpose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.480
meant to make everyone in the audience&nbsp;
feel some secondhand embarrassment.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.920
It's a revealing moment for one of the&nbsp;
main players in this particular saga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.320
but that doesn't make it any easier to watch.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.640
Deputy Inspector Syril Karn is dead&nbsp;
set on finding Cassian Andor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.560
bringing him to justice for killing two&nbsp;
guards on the planet Morlana One. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.280
he gathers a squad of Prelox-Morlana corporate&nbsp;
authority officers to track down Andor. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:42.556
encouraged to give a speech to the troops to rally&nbsp;
them, but his attempt doesn't exactly go well.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:48.409
"There's no room for doubt&nbsp;
on the path to… success."

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:53.120
There are a few reasons why the writers likely&nbsp;
decided to go in this direction with Syril and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.000
include such a painfully embarrassing scene.&nbsp;
In the next episode, Cassian and Luthen Rael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.120
manage to outmaneuver Syril and&nbsp;
his soldiers in a show of battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.920
superiority. So perhaps it's meant&nbsp;
to illustrate his lack of experience.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.040
There is, perhaps, an even deeper meaning behind&nbsp;
the scene, that ties in with Syril's ambitions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.360
how they're at odds with his actual&nbsp;
abilities. In an interview with Total Film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.600
Kyle Soller, who plays Syril, revealed&nbsp;
quite a bit about his character. He said,

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:24.800
"He has an extreme sense of need to&nbsp;
impress, and fill a hole in himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.880
And so that really is about ascending&nbsp;
to the top of whatever field he's in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:34.080
The field he's chosen is one of restriction&nbsp;
and complete control, and one of domination."

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.040
Exactly what that metaphorical hole that he's&nbsp;
trying to fill really is will likely become more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.320
clear as "Andor" progresses, but with the added&nbsp;
context of Soller's interview and what fans have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:50.160
seen of him throughout the first three episodes,&nbsp;
his disastrous speech reveals quite a bit.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.920
Obviously, he wants to impress his&nbsp;
soldiers, but lacks the experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.972
proper words, or charm, or&nbsp;
battle prowess to do so.

00:02:57.033 --> 00:02:58.296
"Best of luck to us all!"

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:00.560
This also makes him a nice foil for Cassian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.480
who is more personable and relatable in&nbsp;
his interactions with the other characters.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:09.440
So while Syril's speech may be a hard scene&nbsp;
to watch, and arguably a top cringe moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.103
in all of "Star Wars," it's also pivotal&nbsp;
in showing viewers who Syril really is.

